    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1073,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1089,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151410,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151411,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151412,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours occur predominantly in the upper&nbsp;third of the vagina and are more common on the posterior wall {18980291; 11812092}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151413,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical features are similar to those of HPV-associated SCC. The presence of simultaneous cervical or vulvar SCC, or a history of such cancers within the past 5&nbsp;years, must be excluded before making a diagnosis of a vaginal primary.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151414,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal SCC constitutes 1&ndash;2% of all female genital tract malignancies. HPV-associated SCC is more common than HPV-independent SCC; the reported proportion of HPV-independent SCC varies from 20% to 30% {19115209; 28369882; 25155250}. Most patients with HPV-independent SCC are postmenopausal; the median patient age at diagnosis is 73&nbsp;years {21166704; 18500261; 2166263}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151415,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151416,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>TP53</em> mutations occur in some cases {22226684}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151417,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is similar to that of HPV-associated SCC.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151418,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In contrast to HPV-associated SCC, HPV-independent SCC is more likely to be of the keratinizing pattern {21166704; 18500261}; however, there is significant morphological overlap, and a reliable distinction cannot be made on morphology alone. p16 negativity&nbsp;/ non&ndash;block-type staining and p53 mutation-type staining are common findings {22226684; 30561092}. Grading is currently not recommended because of the lack of evidence {35703457}.</p>\r\n<p>No established precursor lesion for HPV-independent vaginal SCC has been identified to date; unlike in the cervix and vulva, a category of HPV-independent precursor lesion is not recognized.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151419,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HPV-associated and HPV-independent SCCs are indistinguishable on the basis of cytopathology.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151420,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The absence of HPV may be ascertained by PCR or in&nbsp;situ hybridization for low- and high-risk HPV types.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151421,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential: </em>invasive carcinoma with squamous differentiation; no previous (&le;&nbsp;5&nbsp;years) or concomitant SCC of cervix or vulva; negative/non&ndash;block-type p16 staining and/or negative HPV molecular testing.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151422,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These neoplasms are staged using the FIGO and TNM staging systems.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151423,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 33,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Because of the rarity of these neoplasms, evidence for a different prognosis for HPV-independent compared with HPV-associated SCC is more limited in comparison with that for the vulva. Available evidence shows that HPV-independent SCCs of the vagina have worse overall and progression-free survival compared with HPV-associated SCC, independent of stage and patient age {21666483; 2166263; 23402906; 22226684; 34546565}. Currently, there is no evidence of a difference in survival between p53-mutated and p53-wildtype HPV-independent SCC {34546565}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:13:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]